  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper level ridge persists across the eastern CONUS allowing for \ncontinued heat and humidity to impact the region through Thursday. \nTemperatures at 850mb remain warm in the 20-22C range Wednesday \nbefore lowering slightly into the 18-20C range by Thursday. Daily \nafternoon highs warm into the low 90s on Wednesday and upper \n80s on Thursday. There will be little overnight relief as lows \nsettle in the low 70s each night. Despite the potential for \nearly morning convection, have expanded the Heat Advisory to \ninclude the remaining counties in Northern Ohio for Wednesday. \nConfidence in morning showers and storms remains high, however \nguidance continues to show rapid clearing of lingering cloud \ncover by late Wednesday morning. There should be enough clearing\nto allow for temperatures to warm and heat index values to rise\ninto the upper 90s to lower 100s. Additionally the entire\nforecast area remains under Major HeatRisk (Level 3 of 4) on \nWednesday. Heat lingers into Thursday but confidence is\ncurrently too low to extend the Heat Advisory beyond Wednesday\nnight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA series of ridge riders will continue to crest atop the upper ridge \naxis through Thursday. These shortwaves will provide numerous \nchances for showers and thunderstorms into the end of the week.\nDaily chances for strong to severe storms exist given amble \ninstability (2000 J/kg MLCAPE) and favorable bulk shear (30-35 \nknots) in the warm and moist environment. Some of the latest \nCAMs have hinted at isolated showers and storms entering \nNorthwest Ohio tonight though there is low confidence in any \nstrong to severe storms this evening. Hi-res guidance continues \nto show storms pushing into Northeast Ohio and Northwest \nPennsylvania before daybreak on Wednesday. strong to severe \nstorms are likely with the early morning round, particularly \nwhere the SWODY1 Slight Risk (Level 2 of 5) exists. Primary \nhazards will be damaging wind gusts and large hail. Some \nadditional isolated showers and storms will remain possible in \nthe wake of the morning convection, mainly across eastern zones,\ngiven the continued unstable and moist environment. \n\nBy Wednesday night, a cold front will begin to push southward across \nLake Erie providing another window for strong to severe storms to \nimpact the region. There remains a SWODY2 Slight Risk (Level 2 of 5) \nfor severe thunderstorms on Wednesday. Primary hazards in \nthunderstorms associated with the cold front Wednesday night will be \ndamaging wind gusts and large hail. There is a lower chance for \ntornadoes. Fairly widespread showers and storms will be ongoing \nThursday into Friday. The severe weather threat area shifts further \nsouth to central Ohio by Thursday as the cold front sinks southward. \nTrending drier by the weekend as Canadian high pressure builds over \nthe region. \n\nWith the warm and moist environment, PWATs will range between \n1.5-2 inches. Any thunderstorms that move across the region \nwill be capable of producing heavy rainfall. Localized flooding \nis possible where repeated rounds of thunderstorms occur.",
